
{"Version of Hexed that you are currently running."}
version = version_major + {.} + version_minor + {.} + version_patch
{"
Text of the license under which this copy of Hexed is distributed.
So, to show the license, you can simply do `license` in an interactive session or `print license` in an input file.
"}
license = newline + read {LICENSE.txt}

{"`reference_value` used in \ref air_viscosity ."}
air_visc_ref = 1.716e-5
{"`reference_temperature` used in \ref air_viscosity ."}
air_visc_ref_temp = 273.
{"`offset` used in \ref air_viscosity ."}
air_visc_offset = 111.
{"reference value used in \ref air_conductivity ."}
air_cond_ref = .0241
{"`reference_temperature` used in \ref air_conductivity ."}
air_cond_ref_temp = 273.
{"`offset` used in \ref air_conductivity ."}
air_cond_offset = 194.

{"
Best available Sutherland's Law approximation for the viscosity of air that I am aware of,
which is from White \cite white_viscous (Table 1-2).
To use it this model, set `viscosity_model = air_viscosity`.
\see \ref viscosity_model
"}
air_viscosity = {ref_value = air_visc_ref; ref_temperature = air_visc_ref_temp; offset = air_visc_offset;}

{"
Best available Sutherland's Law approximation for the thermal conductivity of air that I am aware of,
which is from White \cite white_viscous (Table 1-3).
To use it this model, set `conductivity_model = air_conductivity`.
\see \ref conductivity_model

\note Using \ref air_viscosity together with \ref air_conductivity results in a non-constant Prandtl number!
Technically, this is correct---air does not have a constant Prandtl number---but
for reasons I do not fully understand, most people use a constant Prandtl number in their transport models.
To obtain a constant Prandtl number, you can do something like:
```{.unparsed}
conductivity_model = {ref_value = 1.4/0.4*specific_gas_air/.72*air_visc_ref; ref_temperature = air_visc_ref_temp; offset = air_visc_offset}
```
(this works for \f$ \gamma = 1.4 \f$ and \f$ Pr = .72 \f$).
\todo Does this give the _correct_ Prandtl number variation?
I just selected the most credible Sutherland models I could find for viscosity and conductivity independently,
but I am not sure to what extent the difference reflects the true Prandtl number variation
versus disagreement between different authors.
There may be merit to trying to tweak the default viscosity and conductivity models to work better in combination.
"}
air_conductivity = {ref_value = air_cond_ref; ref_temperature = air_cond_ref_temp; offset = air_cond_offset;}

